// Existing project: build knowledge by reverse engineering the code, then confirm it.
// Vars: peer, folder, project, test, approver
claim: KB-1 The architecture, components and data flows of {{project}} are documented from the code
claim: KB-2 How to build, test and run it is documented, and the owner's test check passes as documented
claim: KB-3 Security, privacy and reliability hotspots are listed with file references
claim: KB-4 Someone who knows {{project}} confirmed the knowledge
architecture @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=reverse-engineer save=architecture Reverse-engineer this codebase. Describe its purpose, architecture, main components and their responsibilities, entry points, data stores, external services and dependencies, and the main data flows, with file paths. Concise Markdown.
build @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=build-run-discovery verify={{test}} claims=KB-2 save=build-and-run Find out how to build, test and run this project: commands, prerequisites, configuration and environment variables. Don't change any file. Concise Markdown.
risks after architecture @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=risk-review save=risks List security, privacy (which personal data is handled, where it is stored, who can access it, how long it is kept) and reliability hotspots, each with file references, severity and a suggested next step.
glossary after architecture @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=reverse-engineer save=glossary Write a glossary of the domain terms and code names used in this system.
confirm after architecture,build,risks approve @{{approver}} claims=KB-1,KB-3,KB-4 Check that this knowledge about {{project}} is right: architecture, build and run, risks.